  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">01 — Introduction</span>Introduction to Vulnerability Assessment</h2>

  <p>In the digital age, every connected system — from a simple home router to a corporate data center — carries the risk of being exploited by malicious actors. <strong>Vulnerability Assessment (VA)</strong> is the systematic process of identifying, quantifying, and prioritizing security weaknesses in a computer system, network, or application before attackers find and exploit them.</p>

  <div class="analogy-box">
    <p>Think of your organization's IT infrastructure as a large building. A vulnerability assessment is like hiring a professional inspector who walks through every room, checks every lock, tests every window, and reports back: "Room 3 has a broken latch, the basement door is unlocked, and the rooftop access is wide open." Without that inspection, you wouldn't know what to fix.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>What is a Vulnerability?</h3>
  <p>A <strong>vulnerability</strong> is any weakness in a system's design, implementation, operation, or internal controls that could be exploited by a threat actor to gain unauthorized access, disrupt service, steal data, or cause other harm.</p>

  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>🏗️ Design Vulnerabilities</h4>
      <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--text2);">Flaws baked into the architecture of software or protocols from inception. Example: early Telnet protocol transmitting passwords in plaintext.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>💻 Implementation Vulnerabilities</h4>
      <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--text2);">Bugs introduced during coding. Example: buffer overflow in C programs, SQL injection due to unsanitized input.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>⚙️ Configuration Vulnerabilities</h4>
      <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--text2);">Systems set up incorrectly. Example: leaving default credentials on a router (admin/admin), open SMB shares.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>👤 Human Vulnerabilities</h4>
      <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--text2);">Social engineering targets. Example: phishing emails tricking employees into revealing credentials.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

