<!-- SECTION 2 -->
  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">02 — History</span>History &amp; Evolution of Scanning</h2>

  <p>The field of vulnerability assessment evolved alongside the growth of the internet and the escalating sophistication of cyberattacks. Understanding this evolution helps us appreciate why tools like Nmap and Nessus became industry standards.</p>

  <div class="timeline">
    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
      <div class="timeline-year">1988</div>
      <div class="timeline-title">Morris Worm — The Wake-Up Call</div>
      <div class="timeline-text">The first major internet worm exploited UNIX vulnerabilities (sendmail, fingerd). It infected ~6,000 machines and demonstrated that networked systems were fundamentally insecure. This catalyzed serious investment in security research.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
      <div class="timeline-year">1993</div>
      <div class="timeline-title">SATAN — First Automated VA Tool</div>
      <div class="timeline-text">Security Administrator Tool for Analyzing Networks (SATAN) was released, becoming the first widely used automated vulnerability scanner. It shocked the public but proved that automated scanning was both feasible and necessary.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
      <div class="timeline-year">1997</div>
      <div class="timeline-title">Nmap Born (Gordon Lyon)</div>
      <div class="timeline-text">Fyodor (Gordon Lyon) released Nmap in Phrack magazine. Originally a simple port scanner, Nmap rapidly evolved into the Swiss Army knife of network reconnaissance. Still actively developed today.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
      <div class="timeline-year">1998</div>
      <div class="timeline-title">Nessus Created (Renaud Deraison)</div>
      <div class="timeline-text">Renaud Deraison released Nessus as a free, open-source vulnerability scanner. Its plugin architecture was revolutionary — security researchers could write and share detection scripts, making it community-driven.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
      <div class="timeline-year">2002</div>
      <div class="timeline-title">CVE &amp; NVD Standardization</div>
      <div class="timeline-text">The Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) database and National Vulnerability Database (NVD) became standard references, giving scanners like Nessus a common language for reporting vulnerabilities.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
      <div class="timeline-year">2005</div>
      <div class="timeline-title">Nessus Goes Commercial</div>
      <div class="timeline-text">Tenable Network Security acquired Nessus and changed it to a closed-source, commercial model. This drove the community to fork it as OpenVAS. Nessus Essentials (free tier) still exists for limited use.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
      <div class="timeline-year">2009+</div>
      <div class="timeline-title">Nmap Adds NSE Scripting Engine</div>
      <div class="timeline-text">Nmap's Scripting Engine (NSE) transformed it from a port scanner into a versatile security assessment tool capable of vulnerability detection, exploitation, and network discovery.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="timeline-item">
      <div class="timeline-dot"></div>
      <div class="timeline-year">Today</div>
      <div class="timeline-title">Cloud-Native &amp; AI-Assisted Scanning</div>
      <div class="timeline-text">Modern VA tools integrate with cloud environments (AWS, Azure, GCP), CI/CD pipelines, and increasingly incorporate AI/ML to reduce false positives and predict attack paths. Nessus and Nmap remain foundational to cybersecurity workflows.</div>
    </div>
  </div>


