    </div>
  </div>


<!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">05 — Scan Types</span>Nmap Scan Types Explained</h2>

  <p>Nmap offers a rich variety of scanning techniques, each suited for different scenarios. Understanding these is essential for both offensive security professionals and defenders.</p>

  <h3>TCP Connect Scan (-sT)</h3>
  <div class="card nmap-card">
    <p>The most basic scan type. Nmap asks the OS to establish a full TCP 3-way handshake (SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK) with every target port. Does not require root privileges but is noisy and easily detected by IDS.</p>
    <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:12px;">
      <pre><span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">-sT</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.1</span>
<span class="output">PORT     STATE  SERVICE
22/tcp   open   ssh
80/tcp   open   http
443/tcp  open   https</span></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>SYN Stealth Scan (-sS) — "Half-Open"</h3>
  <div class="card nmap-card">
    <p>The default and most popular scan. Sends SYN packet; if SYN-ACK received, port is open, then Nmap sends RST (never completes handshake). Faster and less detectable than TCP connect. Requires root/administrator privileges.</p>
    <div class="diagram-wrapper" style="margin:12px 0 0;">
      <div class="diagram-title">SYN Scan Handshake Logic</div>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 600 160" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:600px;width:100%;">
        <text x="100" y="25" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="12" fill="#60a5fa" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">NMAP</text>
        <text x="500" y="25" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="12" fill="#34d399" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">TARGET</text>
        <line x1="100" y1="35" x2="100" y2="150" stroke="#60a5fa" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.4"></line>
        <line x1="500" y1="35" x2="500" y2="150" stroke="#34d399" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.4"></line>
        <!-- SYN -->
        <line x1="100" y1="60" x2="490" y2="60" stroke="#fbbf24" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></line>
        <text x="300" y="55" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" fill="#fbbf24" text-anchor="middle">SYN</text>
        <!-- SYN-ACK -->
        <line x1="490" y1="95" x2="110" y2="95" stroke="#34d399" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></line>
        <text x="300" y="90" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" fill="#34d399" text-anchor="middle">SYN-ACK (Port Open!)</text>
        <!-- RST -->
        <line x1="100" y1="130" x2="490" y2="130" stroke="#f87171" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arrow)" stroke-dasharray="5,3"></line>
        <text x="300" y="125" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" fill="#f87171" text-anchor="middle">RST (drops connection silently)</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:12px;">
      <pre><span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">-sS</span> <span class="flag">-p</span> <span class="string">1-1000</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.1</span></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>UDP Scan (-sU)</h3>
  <div class="card nmap-card">
    <p>Scans UDP ports. UDP is connectionless so detecting open ports is harder — Nmap sends UDP packet and waits. If ICMP "port unreachable" is received → closed. No response → open or filtered. Very slow (can take hours). Critical for finding DNS (53), SNMP (161), DHCP (67/68) services.</p>
    <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:12px;">
      <pre><span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">-sU</span> <span class="flag">--top-ports</span> <span class="string">20</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.1</span>
<span class="comment"># Tip: Combine with -sS for both TCP and UDP</span>
<span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">-sS</span> <span class="flag">-sU</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.1</span></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Version Detection (-sV)</h3>
  <div class="card nmap-card">
    <p>After finding open ports, Nmap probes them to determine the service name and version number. This is crucial — knowing it's Apache 2.2.17 (vulnerable) vs. Apache 2.4.52 (patched) changes the risk calculation entirely.</p>
    <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:12px;">
      <pre><span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">-sV</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.1</span>
<span class="output">PORT    STATE SERVICE  VERSION
22/tcp  open  ssh      OpenSSH 7.2p2 Ubuntu 4ubuntu2.10
80/tcp  open  http     Apache httpd 2.4.18
3306/tcp open mysql   MySQL 5.7.33-0ubuntu0.16.04.1</span></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>OS Detection (-O)</h3>
  <div class="card nmap-card">
    <p>Nmap analyzes subtle differences in network protocol implementations (TCP window sizes, TTL values, IP ID sequences, IPID behavior) to fingerprint the operating system. Requires at least one open and one closed port.</p>
    <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:12px;">
      <pre><span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">-O</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.1</span>
<span class="output">OS details: Linux 3.2 - 4.9
Network Distance: 1 hop
OS CPE: cpe:/o:linux:linux_kernel:3</span></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Aggressive Scan (-A)</h3>
  <div class="card nmap-card">
    <p>Enables OS detection, version detection, script scanning, and traceroute. Noisy but comprehensive. Equivalent to -sV -O -sC --traceroute.</p>
    <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:12px;">
      <pre><span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">-A</span> <span class="flag">-T4</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.0/24</span>
<span class="comment"># -T4: Aggressive timing for faster scan</span></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Timing Templates (-T0 to -T5)</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <table class="comparison-table" style="margin:0;">
      <tbody><tr>
        <th>Template</th><th>Name</th><th>Use Case</th><th>Speed</th>
      </tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag">-T0</span></td><td>Paranoid</td><td>IDS evasion — very slow</td><td>⬛⬛⬛⬛⬛</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag">-T1</span></td><td>Sneaky</td><td>IDS evasion — slow</td><td>🟦⬛⬛⬛⬛</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag">-T2</span></td><td>Polite</td><td>Low bandwidth impact</td><td>🟦🟦⬛⬛⬛</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag">-T3</span></td><td>Normal</td><td>Default</td><td>🟦🟦🟦⬛⬛</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag">-T4</span></td><td>Aggressive</td><td>Fast, reliable network</td><td>🟦🟦🟦🟦⬛</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag">-T5</span></td><td>Insane</td><td>Maximum speed, may miss results</td><td>🟦🟦🟦🟦🟦</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
  </div>


<!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">06 — NSE</span>Nmap Scripting Engine (NSE)</h2>

  <p>The <strong>Nmap Scripting Engine (NSE)</strong> is one of Nmap's most powerful features. It allows users to write (and share) Lua scripts to automate a wide variety of networking tasks. NSE transforms Nmap from a port scanner into a comprehensive vulnerability assessment tool.</p>

  <h3>NSE Script Categories</h3>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>🔐 auth</h4>
      <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text2);">Tests authentication credentials — default passwords, anonymous access. Example: <code>ftp-anon</code>, <code>http-auth</code></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>📢 broadcast</h4>
      <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text2);">Discovers hosts by broadcasting on the local network. Useful for finding devices not responding to unicast probes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>🔨 brute</h4>
      <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text2);">Performs brute-force password auditing against authentication services (SSH, FTP, HTTP, SMB).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>🔎 discovery</h4>
      <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text2);">Actively queries public registries, SNMP devices, and directory services to learn more about targets.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>⚡ exploit</h4>
      <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text2);">Actively exploits vulnerabilities. Example: <code>smb-vuln-ms17-010</code> (EternalBlue/WannaCry check).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>🛡️ vuln</h4>
      <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text2);">Checks for specific vulnerabilities without exploiting them. Example: <code>ssl-heartbleed</code>, <code>http-shellshock</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Common NSE Commands</h3>
  <div class="code-block">
    <div class="code-label">$ NSE Usage Examples</div>
    <pre><span class="comment"># Run default scripts (equivalent to -sC)</span>
<span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">--script</span> <span class="string">default</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.1</span>

<span class="comment"># Check for Heartbleed SSL vulnerability</span>
<span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">--script</span> <span class="string">ssl-heartbleed</span> <span class="flag">-p</span> <span class="string">443</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.1</span>

<span class="comment"># Check EternalBlue (MS17-010 / WannaCry)</span>
<span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">--script</span> <span class="string">smb-vuln-ms17-010</span> <span class="flag">-p</span> <span class="string">445</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.1</span>

<span class="comment"># Run all vulnerability scripts</span>
<span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">--script</span> <span class="string">vuln</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.1</span>

<span class="comment"># HTTP enumeration</span>
<span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">--script</span> <span class="string">http-enum</span> <span class="flag">-p</span> <span class="string">80,443</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.1</span>

<span class="comment"># Detect OS and run safe scripts with version</span>
<span class="cmd">nmap</span> <span class="flag">-sV</span> <span class="flag">-sC</span> <span class="flag">-O</span> <span class="string">192.168.1.0/24</span></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="info-box">
    <div class="icon">📁</div>
    <p>NSE scripts are stored at <strong>/usr/share/nmap/scripts/</strong> on Linux systems. You can view all available scripts with: <code>ls /usr/share/nmap/scripts/ | grep vuln</code></p>
  </div>


