  <h3>Nessus Plugin Architecture</h3>
  <p>Nessus plugins are individual scripts (written in NASL — Nessus Attack Scripting Language) that perform specific checks. Each plugin targets one specific vulnerability or configuration issue.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <div class="code-label">NASL — Example Plugin Logic (Simplified)</div>
    <pre><span class="comment"># Plugin 97833: MS17-010 EternalBlue Check</span>
<span class="string">name</span>: <span class="output">"MS17-010: Security Update for Windows SMB Server"</span>
<span class="string">family</span>: <span class="output">"Windows: Microsoft Bulletins"</span>
<span class="string">cvss_base_score</span>: <span class="output">9.3</span>
<span class="string">cve</span>: <span class="output">CVE-2017-0143, CVE-2017-0144</span>

<span class="comment"># The plugin connects to port 445, sends crafted SMB</span>
<span class="comment"># packets, and checks if the system responds in a way</span>
<span class="comment"># that indicates it's vulnerable to EternalBlue</span></pre>
  </div>

  <h3>Plugin Families</h3>
  <ul class="styled">
    <li><strong>Windows:</strong> Microsoft patches (MS Bulletins), Active Directory, IIS, Exchange vulnerabilities</li>
    <li><strong>Unix/Linux:</strong> Kernel vulnerabilities, package-level CVEs for Debian/RHEL/Ubuntu/CentOS</li>
    <li><strong>Network Infrastructure:</strong> Cisco IOS, Juniper JunOS, Palo Alto PAN-OS, F5 vulnerabilities</li>
    <li><strong>Web Servers:</strong> Apache, Nginx, IIS, Tomcat, Node.js — HTTP header issues, server-side vulnerabilities</li>
    <li><strong>Databases:</strong> MySQL, MSSQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL — default accounts, unpatched CVEs</li>
    <li><strong>SSL/TLS:</strong> Weak cipher suites, expired certificates, POODLE, BEAST, Heartbleed, DROWN</li>
  </ul>


