  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">11 — Case Studies</span>Real-World Case Studies</h2>

  <h3>📖 Case Study 1: WannaCry Ransomware (2017)</h3>
  <div class="card orange-card">
    <p><strong>Background:</strong> In May 2017, WannaCry ransomware infected over 230,000 computers in 150 countries, causing billions in damages. It exploited <strong>EternalBlue (MS17-010)</strong> — a vulnerability in Windows SMB protocol (port 445).</p>
    <p><strong>How VA Tools Could Have Helped:</strong></p>
    <ul class="styled" style="margin:8px 0;">
      <li>Running <strong>Nmap</strong> with <code>--script smb-vuln-ms17-010</code> would have identified all vulnerable Windows hosts exposing port 445</li>
      <li><strong>Nessus Plugin #97833</strong> directly checks for MS17-010 and flags it as Critical (CVSS 9.3) with immediate remediation guidance</li>
      <li>Organizations that had conducted regular VA scans and applied the March 2017 Microsoft patch were unaffected</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Regular vulnerability scanning with patch verification would have prevented catastrophic damage. NHS (UK) lost £92M and had 19,000 medical appointments cancelled — all preventable.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>📖 Case Study 2: Heartbleed OpenSSL Bug (2014)</h3>
  <div class="card red-card">
    <p><strong>Background:</strong> CVE-2014-0160 (Heartbleed) was a critical flaw in OpenSSL that allowed attackers to read server memory — potentially exposing private keys, passwords, and session cookies. It affected approximately two-thirds of all web servers.</p>
    <p><strong>VA Tool Response:</strong></p>
    <ul class="styled" style="margin:8px 0;">
      <li><strong>Nmap NSE script</strong> <code>ssl-heartbleed</code> was published within 24 hours of disclosure, allowing rapid scanning of all HTTPS hosts</li>
      <li>Nessus added Plugin #73564 that day, enabling mass scanning of corporate networks</li>
      <li>Security teams used these tools to scan thousands of servers overnight and prioritize patches</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Lesson:</strong> VA tools enable rapid response to newly disclosed vulnerabilities. Organizations with mature VA programs patched Heartbleed in days; others took weeks.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>📖 Case Study 3: University Network Audit</h3>
  <div class="card nessus-card">
    <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A mid-sized university wanted to assess security before accreditation. They had 3,000+ devices across faculty, student, and administrative networks.</p>
    <p><strong>Methodology Used:</strong></p>
    <div class="step-flow" style="margin-top:12px;">
      <div class="step-item">
        <div class="step-num-circle" style="width:30px;height:30px;font-size:12px;">1</div>
        <div class="step-content"><strong>Nmap Phase:</strong> <p>Ran <code>nmap -sn 10.0.0.0/16</code> to discover 3,247 live hosts across all VLANs in under 10 minutes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-item">
        <div class="step-num-circle" style="width:30px;height:30px;font-size:12px;">2</div>
        <div class="step-content"><strong>Nessus Phase:</strong> <p>Imported Nmap results into Nessus and ran credentialed scans on servers, uncredentialed on student devices.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-item">
        <div class="step-num-circle" style="width:30px;height:30px;font-size:12px;">3</div>
        <div class="step-content"><strong>Results:</strong> <p>Found 847 critical/high findings: 234 unpatched Windows systems, 12 servers with default credentials, FTP with anonymous login on 3 servers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-item">
        <div class="step-num-circle" style="width:30px;height:30px;font-size:12px;">4</div>
        <div class="step-content"><strong>Outcome:</strong> <p>90-day remediation window. Follow-up scan showed 94% of critical findings resolved. University passed accreditation.</p></div>
