  <h3>📖 Case Study 4: Hospital PCI DSS Compliance</h3>
  <div class="card green-card">
    <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A hospital processing credit card payments needed to pass PCI DSS assessment. Nessus was used to perform compliance auditing across payment processing systems.</p>
    <p><strong>What Nessus Found:</strong> TLS 1.0 still enabled on payment gateway servers (PCI DSS 6.5.4 violation), default SNMP community strings ("public") on network devices, and several Windows servers with missing patches older than 90 days.</p>
    <p><strong>Result:</strong> After remediation validated by Nessus rescans, the hospital achieved PCI DSS compliance, avoiding fines of $5,000–$100,000 per month that would have been levied.</p>
  </div>


<!-- SECTION 12 -->
  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">12 — Pros &amp; Cons</span>Advantages &amp; Disadvantages</h2>

  <h3>Nmap — Pros &amp; Cons</h3>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card green-card">
      <h4>✅ Advantages of Nmap</h4>
      <ul class="styled" style="margin:0;">
        <li>Completely free and open source (GPL license)</li>
        <li>Extremely fast — scan thousands of hosts in minutes</li>
        <li>Highly flexible — hundreds of command-line options</li>
        <li>Cross-platform: Linux, Windows, macOS</li>
        <li>Extensible via NSE Lua scripting</li>
        <li>Massive community, excellent documentation</li>
        <li>Low false positive rate for port states</li>
        <li>Can be integrated into automated pipelines</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card red-card">
      <h4>❌ Disadvantages of Nmap</h4>
      <ul class="styled" style="margin:0;">
        <li>Steep learning curve — CLI-only (Zenmap GUI limited)</li>
        <li>Limited built-in vulnerability database</li>
        <li>No compliance reporting capabilities</li>
        <li>NSE vuln scripts can have false positives</li>
        <li>No credentialed scanning for deep host audit</li>
        <li>Reports require manual interpretation</li>
        <li>UDP scanning is very slow</li>
        <li>Not suited as standalone enterprise VA tool</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Nessus — Pros &amp; Cons</h3>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card green-card">
      <h4>✅ Advantages of Nessus</h4>
      <ul class="styled" style="margin:0;">
        <li>100,000+ plugins updated daily by Tenable</li>
        <li>Intuitive web-based GUI — easy for beginners</li>
        <li>Credentialed scanning for deep host analysis</li>
        <li>Built-in compliance frameworks (PCI, HIPAA, NIST)</li>
        <li>Detailed remediation guidance for each finding</li>
        <li>Rich, exportable reports (PDF, CSV, HTML)</li>
        <li>Low false positive rate with credentialed scans</li>
        <li>Integrates with SIEM, ticketing, and IT systems</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card red-card">
      <h4>❌ Disadvantages of Nessus</h4>
      <ul class="styled" style="margin:0;">
        <li>Expensive Professional license ($3,390/yr+)</li>
        <li>Free Essentials limited to 16 IPs only</li>
        <li>Scanning can be slow on large networks</li>
        <li>Resource-intensive — needs dedicated scanner system</li>
        <li>Can be disruptive to fragile systems (DoS risk)</li>
        <li>Closed source — limited customization</li>
        <li>Generates alert storms — requires analyst tuning</li>
        <li>Cloud-dependent for plugin updates</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Ethical &amp; Legal Considerations</h3>
  <div class="info-box" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a22,#45000022);border:1px solid #dc2626;border-radius:12px;">
    <div class="icon">⚖️</div>
    <div>
      <p style="color:var(--text);font-weight:700;margin-bottom:6px;">Critical Warning: Always Get Written Authorization</p>
      <p>Running Nmap or Nessus against systems you do not own or do not have explicit written permission to scan is a <strong>federal crime</strong> in the United States (Computer Fraud and Abuse Act), and illegal in most jurisdictions worldwide. Always obtain a signed Rules of Engagement (RoE) document before scanning. Even scanning your own employer's systems without authorization can result in legal consequences.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

